{"kind":"request","text":"Builder: design the adopted general capability and context interface for the shared core, with enforceable bounds and a concrete path to Chess adoption.","body":{"branch":"request/capability-context-design","conditions":"Prepare the bounded I7 capability/context decision from current main9280be8b, in its own worktree. Governing scope is sections3/5 of Hugh-adopted Gitseq notes/2026-09-04-tailapps-jsonataddl-adoption.md@860ee61a07aa753dcbc2d50e74da2b7b6547625b and the six rules/eight admission gates of notes/2026-08-27-jsonata-ddl-stable-extensions.md at that same immutable source. I1 is delivered; I7 is not technically dependent on I5. Define the smallest general core/provider/context boundary with explicit per-program declarations/allowlists, meaning-based contract identifiers and canonical identity participation, deterministic input/output/work/memory/recursion bounds, argument/result validation and fixed failure diagnostics. No ambient I/O/state, provider effects or raw secret-bearing diagnostics. Domain refusal stays data; malformed/unavailable/panicking/exhausted providers cause interpretation failure and preserve the prior frontier/transaction. Make exact-record context order, revocation/scope/expiry and fail-closed admission concrete. Reuse existing JSONata and confined writer/authorizer mechanisms; preserve current pins, topology and host transaction ownership unless an essential change is justified. Reconcile the API with I5's declared-input design without claiming its draft has landed. Do not build a generic plugin framework, restore historical evaluation or implement Chess inside the shared core. Chess is the first consumer, not the specification. Name enforceable metering, conformance and omission-sensitive tests for all eight gates, including the two-implementation condition only when supported alternatives exist; expose any unsupported provider as an admission blocker. Include short core/release/host/provider/Chess stages with